  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Currently, static world rows promote the same worlds since years that everyone already knows, no matter how outdated or uninteresting they are today. \nIt doesn't seem like these columns are actively maintained and used to help promote new and interesting worlds to users. Instead, it feels more like the menu rather wants to keep promoting the same worlds since years, even if nobody uses them anymore.\nAn extreme example to this is the Udon world row, where some cheap sandbox worlds are still promoted today while huge Udon world projects are not in there. I know first hand that VRChat staff knows some of these worlds, but they simply don't add them in there and I don't understand why. This world row is just frozen since months now and there is a lot of cool Udon stuff out there.\nIf new worlds are added to a row, they are only added to the very end of that row, which again just keeps the same old worlds active instead of actively showing new ones.\nGiven how small the Udon community is, it's easy to find good new Udon world projects and promote them in there, which would also give their creators appreciation (the only reward there is for their free work).\nIn general, VRChat should better use these world rows to promote new worlds to users and keep their platform active. As a user, I often feel unable to find good new worlds myself and these world rows could be a good tool to promote new creators, appreciate their work and keep the game interesting. If the world rows are almost static since years, they don't provide any user value to me and only force me to scroll down each time to the only useful row \"active my plattform\".\nThe best would be if VRChat updates the rows e.g. each week (completely re-evaluating what's still in there and looking for potential new worlds based on user activities and findings). This would ensure that promoted worlds that are not used anymore are replaced in the promotion rows by new, currently active worlds (and also worlds that VRChat sees at potentially interesting).",
